db/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151768 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ef66779-081f-5eda-82b4-3ed4176bc616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152029 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152029 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bcc7bb9-1b5e-5858-811d-1e5c14f0bc29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152029 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_6[t])=6+1=7.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_n[t])=n+1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152030 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152030 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9?$\n\nI. $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n\nII. $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n\nIII. $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1ae146f-04ae-5662-9ea3-178a23b3c366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152030 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}\n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152031 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152031 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$\n\nII. $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $50 \\times 100$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152031 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d2ba662-f58e-56ec-bf87-c1f26c12c1c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152031 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$ and $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^{100}) = 100\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})) = 50\\cdot 100 = 5\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\},\\quad n\\in \\mathbb N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n a_i t^i = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a_i = 0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots, n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in \\mathbb N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152032 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152032 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 6$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61fa85c6-5b27-5f81-a7ba-363eb68e8d3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152032 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of $\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$ contains $3\\cdot 2= 6$ elements, and is given by\n\n![](images/8a/8a50f2d0ea7a7d24ca498b7e326bd66fb9afcfc089159146f07018c96fecfaf8.svg)\n\nHowever, we're asked to find the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ not $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIf we were to repeat the same process above for the vector space $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ we would have $30$ matrices in our basis, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{5\\times 6}(\\mathbb{R})) = 5\\cdot 6 =30.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})) = n\\cdot m.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186152029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152219 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186152030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152220 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152221 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152221 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152221 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152221 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152221 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152221 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152221 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175