edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1045 "Dimension in Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152367 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "656cb00f-9a01-5d98-b674-14d57dda7e9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152876 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152876 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152876 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n1. $ℓ^{2}$, the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the squares of the terms are finite\n2. $R_{2}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$\n3. $M_{100 \\cdot 1000}(R)$, the vector space of all $100 \\cdot 1000$ matrices over $R$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152876 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a7f694e-9eb9-56c4-8f1f-0236761451e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153161 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153161 #edb/ref 1061 653421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153161 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c281721-48ee-5e84-901c-07d72bf77044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153161 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153162 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153162 #edb/ref 1061 653422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153162 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "977a3893-c3c0-5448-ab25-3271a4a3a0ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153162 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153163 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153163 #edb/ref 1061 653428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153163 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32c06dcf-98e2-59eb-83b8-eaa3a8573b55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153163 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153164 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153164 #edb/ref 1061 653431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153164 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "17e7956b-85c4-535f-b3e9-ef87f6a27c75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153164 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153165 #edb/ref 1061 653432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153165 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a4fa43c-f576-546c-b417-c6b36b6e838e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153165 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153166 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153166 #edb/ref 1061 653425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153166 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb8f5e62-9e00-5ea4-b454-29f57b6bd487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153166 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153408 #edb/ref 1064 "The standard basis of the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ with degree at most $2$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2\\}.\n$$\n\nAnother basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ t^2-1, \\: t+3, \\: 2t-1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn general, vector spaces have infinitely many bases. \n\nLet's now consider the following theorem:\n\n*If a basis of a vector space $V$ has $n$ elements, then every basis of $V$ has $n$ elements.*\n\nThe number $n$ is called the **dimension** of the vector space, and we write $\\textrm{dim}(V)=n.$\n\nSo, since the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ has $3$ elements, all of its bases have $3$ elements, and we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_2[t])=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153408 #edb/ref 1065 4971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153408 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91a87b01-a3a9-5fd9-865d-2e7fa9fb4636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153408 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153409 #edb/ref 1064 "When a vector space $V$ has a finite dimension $\\textrm{dim}(V) = n < \\infty,$ the vector space is **finite-dimensional**.\n\nSome vector spaces can have arbitrarily large linearly independent sets. In such cases, the vector space is **infinite-dimensional**.\n\nAn example of an infinite-dimensional vector space is the space of all continuous functions $C(\\mathbb{R})$ defined on $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTo understand why $C(\\mathbb{R})$ is an infinite-dimensional vector space, we note that for any positive integer $n,$ the following set of continuous functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n x_i t^i = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $x_i=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots,n.$ Since $n$ can be arbitrarily large, our vector space cannot have finite dimension." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153409 #edb/ref 1065 4972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153409 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cdc61d9a-3a5b-5d82-b2f8-17ebc848ac95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153409 #edb/ref 1067 "Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462490 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462491 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462492 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243976 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $3?$\n- **I.** ${R}^{2}$\n- **II.** ${R}_{2}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$\n- **III.** ${\\text{M}}_{3}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 3$ matrices over $R$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf92f8df-b777-508f-9358-1b7029534cba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243976 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2}[t])=2+1=3.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3}(R))={3}^{2}=9\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462493 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462494 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462495 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243977 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $20?$\n- **I.** ${R}_{20}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $20$\n- **II.** ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- **III.** ${R}^{20}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186243977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19d4d0ee-ad5b-53e7-956e-b02eb6cf177b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243977 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{20}[t])=20+1=21\\ne 20.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R))=4\\cdot 5=20.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{20})=20.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358043 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358043 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358043 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21209143-d6f3-527a-95fc-698f6fe33a0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358550 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358550 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358550 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5bddb206-b7cb-5ec9-a288-f2949be70a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358956 #edb/ref 1080 "20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358956 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358956 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8a3475b2-1900-543b-9144-6a374fa7f5a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359280 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359280 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359280 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b011e497-97d8-5a66-bc41-ff74a3cf84e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359580 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359580 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359580 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d0d5201c-0d42-5c30-b6c4-c732698b84a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360329 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360329 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360329 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360329 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186359280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360329 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c2fb2713-16be-554b-924a-e6711ddefc4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470488 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470489 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470490 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360589 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360589 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360589 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n- **I.** ${R}^{200}$\n- **II.** $R[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$\n- **III.** ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 200$ matrices over $R$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ccb2591-5640-5714-9681-7f41d3b4bf27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360589 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $R[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{a}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $R[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $R[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R)$ and ${R}^{200}$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200}(R))={200}^{2}=40\\,000,\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{200})=200\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397863 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of ${R}_{20}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $20?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "125dfbaf-7832-535b-889e-b97a19233f9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397863 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{20}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{20}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{20}[t])=20+1=21.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397864 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397864 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397864 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186397869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397864 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7e8a6722-bfae-5fc1-a77c-d568d26da8e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397865 #edb/ref 1080 "20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397865 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397865 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c3f89b7d-1586-5d0d-aa32-fad10cd50db7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397866 #edb/ref 1080 "22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397866 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397866 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cd75d502-bffc-